## A spa for young adults? The idea is only just taking shape

Ask any thirty-something what the word "spa cure" brings to mind and you will hear much the same answer: a slow promenade, a blanket over the knees, an hour-long conversation about blood pressure. A **spa for young adults** still sounds faintly like a contradiction — except that this picture no longer matches what actually happens in Mariánské Lázně. On the colonnade today you will pass runners, cyclists coming back from the Slavkov Forest, and guests who have travelled here for three nights to switch off their phones.

The shift is not a marketing pose. It is the logical consequence of what work, sleep and the nervous system have done to a generation raised on push notifications — and of the fact that a spa stay no longer has to last three weeks.

## Where the blanket-over-the-knees image came from

The stereotype has a real basis. The classic Czech spa cure is a medical procedure: a doctor submits a proposal, the health insurer approves either comprehensive or contributory spa care, and the stay runs to roughly three weeks. A model like that naturally attracts people who already have a diagnosis — and they tend to be older.

Alongside that "grand" cure, however, there has always been a second route: the self-paying stay, shorter, looser, with no approval loop. And that route has grown noticeably stronger in recent years. According to CzechTourism, Czech spa accommodation establishments welcomed 982,000 guests in 2025 — 556,000 domestic and more than 426,000 from abroad — and one of the clearest trends cited is rising interest in shorter "trial" stays, which spas have met with weekend and themed packages.

The short stay is precisely the format a younger guest can manage: it requires neither negotiating three weeks of leave nor waiting for an authorisation. Arrive on Friday, leave on Sunday or Tuesday — and find out whether it suits you.

