## Burnout: the subject that opened new doors for the spa

The strongest reason a spa stay now occurs to people in their thirties and forties is a tiredness that sleep does not fix. In 2019 the World Health Organization included burn-out in the eleventh revision of the International Classification of Diseases under code QD85 — and it is worth noting exactly how. It is neither a disease nor a mental disorder; ICD-11 describes it as an *occupational phenomenon*, a syndrome resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. It shows up in three dimensions: energy depletion, increased mental distance from or cynicism towards one's job, and reduced professional efficacy.

That distinction is not pedantry. A spa stay does not "cure" burnout — such a promise would be dishonest. What a spa town offers is an environment in which recovery stops being another calendar entry and simply becomes the shape of the day: regular sleep, movement outdoors, treatments at fixed times, meals at fixed times, no decisions about what comes next. That rhythm is, paradoxically, the one thing an over-scheduled person cannot set up alone. We have written about it in more depth in our article on [burnout prevention at the spa](/en/magazine/burnout-prevention-spa).

If it is mainly the disconnecting part that appeals, there is a more deliberate version — a [digital detox at the spa](/en/magazine/digital-detox-at-the-spa-how-to-truly-disconnect), where the switched-off phone is part of the programme rather than an accident.

## What a younger guest actually does between treatments

This is where the stereotype breaks fastest. Mariánské Lázně sits at around 630 metres above sea level, surrounded by the Slavkov Forest protected landscape area, which makes the town chiefly an enormous sports ground with spa infrastructure in the middle.

- **Golf with a royal backstory.** The oldest golf course in the Czech Republic was opened on 21 August 1905 by King Edward VII in person; the original nine holes were extended to eighteen in 1923, and in 2003 the club was granted the right to use the title "Royal". The course lies at roughly 787 metres — the views come included with the green fee.
- **Disc golf for nothing.** A public disc golf course occupies the grounds of the former barracks at Na Voře (Ke Kasárnám street). You need nothing more than a disc and a free afternoon.
- **Bike and boots.** Some ninety kilometres of waymarked routes ring the town, running through forest and on to neighbouring towns. If you would rather not pedal, you can cover the same landscape slowly and deliberately — the principle behind [forest bathing](/en/magazine/forest-bathing-mindfulness-slavkov) works particularly well here.
- **Skiing in winter.** The Mariánky ski area offers runs of several difficulty grades totalling around 1,450 metres, with a cable car and two lifts carrying up to 2,500 people an hour.
- **Sauna as a social ritual.** The extensive sauna worlds at the Ensana spa hotels are, for many younger guests, the gateway to the whole stay — you begin with a sauna ritual and end up at a spring.

For the full picture, see our article on [sport in Mariánské Lázně](/en/magazine/sport-v-marianskych-laznich).

## The springs: less ritual, more respect

Sooner or later you will end up at the colonnade — and one rule is worth knowing in advance. Mariánské Lázně has around forty cold mineral springs within the town itself and roughly a hundred more in the surrounding area; these are acidulous waters at 7 to 10 °C all year round. The Cross Spring and the Ferdinand Spring are traditionally associated with metabolic complaints, the Rudolph Spring with the kidneys and urinary tract, the Forest Spring with the respiratory tract.

The drinking cure, though, is **not a drinks dispenser**. It is a therapeutic procedure, and its composition, timing and volume are always set individually by a spa physician according to your diagnosis. The local Glauber's-salt springs have a laxative effect, so "the more the better" is an especially poor idea here. Tasting from a spa cup as you stroll the colonnade is of course part of the experience — but a cure in the therapeutic sense begins with a doctor, not with a spring.

By the same token, the most precious thing in Mariánské Lázně is not a swimming pool but a bath: carbon-dioxide baths and the natural CO₂ gas from the Maria Spring, used at the Ensana houses, are among the treatments that brought people here two hundred years ago.

## When to come

Younger guests rarely get to choose their leave, so the calendar is worth a glance:

- **The Singing Fountain** on the main colonnade plays from 30 April to 31 October. In season it sounds daily on every odd hour from 7 a.m. to 7 p.m., and again at 9 p.m. and 10 p.m. with a coloured light projection.
- **The Chopin Festival** runs from 14 to 22 August in 2026 (its 67th edition), with concerts at venues including the Town Theatre, the Chopin House and the Casino Social House. It is the liveliest week of the spa year.
- **The off-season** — late autumn, January and February — is the quietest and cheapest. Springs without a queue, forests without people, treatments without a wait.

## Getting here without losing a day

For younger visitors, accessibility often decides the matter. Trains leave Prague's main station for Mariánské Lázně roughly every two hours; the fastest is the Pendolino, which runs twice a day, and the journey takes in the order of two and a half hours. Once you are here a car is more hindrance than help — the historic centre can be crossed on foot in twenty minutes.

That leaves the money. A short self-paying break works out very differently from a three-week cure, and the range is wide; indicative prices and what is usually included are covered in our overview of [what a spa week costs in Marienbad](/en/magazine/spa-week-cost-marienbad).

## What to take from this

Spas are not for seniors — they are for people who need to slow down, and a forty-year-old with a management calendar now registers that need as urgently as a seventy-year-old with arthritis. The only difference is what each takes home: one an approved course of treatment, the other three days after which her head has stopped humming for the first time in a year.

Mariánské Lázně manages both — which may well be its most important qualification for the twenty-first century.

